{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "Bellingham's main line -- the Seattle-Vancouver BC corridor, which had no coverage on this map at all despite still carrying Amtrak Cascades today. Bellingham (then the separate towns of Whatcom, Sehome, Fairhaven and Bellingham, consolidated into one city in 1903) got its rail connection from Nelson Bennett's Fairhaven and Southern Railroad, incorporated November 27 1888: trains began running from Fairhaven on February 1 1890, the line to Sedro was finished June 28 1890, and the extension north from Fairhaven to Blaine on the Canadian border was built between August 16 and October 25 1890. The Great Northern acquired the franchise in June 1890. On February 14 1891 a golden spike at Fairhaven joined the F&S to the Seattle and Montana coming up from the south, and on the same day two special trains met at the border to celebrate the last spikes of the New Westminster Southern Railway on the Canadian side. The final link near Stanwood was spiked on October 12 1891, and by late 1891 there was a through Seattle-Fairhaven-Vancouver route. GN ran the Puget Sounders and then, from June 1950, the streamlined International over it; the last International ran May 1 1971 when Amtrak took over. Amtrak's Pacific International -- its first international train -- ran 1972 to September 30 1981, after which Vancouver service lapsed entirely for fourteen years, returning May 26 1995 as the Mount Baker International and folded into the Amtrak Cascades brand in 1998. Bellingham's GN depot dated from a 1902 track relocation and served until 1969; today's Fairhaven Station is a vintage warehouse renovated in 1995.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairhaven_and_Southern_Railroad (incorporated November 27 1888; partial line operating from Fairhaven February 1 1890; Fairhaven-Sedro completed June 28 1890, 50-minute run; extended north from Fairhaven to Blaine August 16 - October 25 1890; 16-mile Sedro-Cokedale coal branch December 6 1890; Great Northern acquired the franchise June 1890 making it an operating subsidiary; golden spike ceremony at Fairhaven February 14 1891 connecting to the Seattle and Montana; part of Seattle and Montana service by December 7 1891; ICC listed 51 miles June 1891, 41 miles June 1893; the Seattle and Montana Railroad Company, incorporated March 29 1898, purchased the F&S the following day)", "https://www.historylink.org/File/8688 (last spike driven October 12 1891 on the west side of the trestle over Nevill's Slough south of Woodland, east of Stanwood, completing the railroad between Seattle and British Columbia; February 14 1891 two special trains met at the border to celebrate the driving of the last spikes completing the New Westminster and Southern Railway connection; the Great Northern link from Seattle to Fairhaven was completed late in 1891)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_(GN_train) (King Street Station Seattle to Pacific Central Station Vancouver BC, 154 miles, six intermediate stops -- Everett, Mount Vernon, Bellingham, Blaine, White Rock and New Westminster; streamlined International debuted June 1950 replacing the older Puget Sounders; daily, average journey 3 hours 55 minutes; three streamlined trainsets on multiple daily round trips at first; evening International discontinued by 1960; morning service ended by 1969, leaving only afternoon trains through the 1970 Burlington Northern merger; final trains May 1 1971 when Amtrak commenced; Amtrak's Pacific International followed 1972-1981; service resumed 1995 as the Mount Baker International; part of Amtrak Cascades using leased Talgo Pendular equipment since 1998)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacific_International (Amtrak's first international train, Seattle-Vancouver BC 1972-1981, 156 miles, 6 stops, about 4 hours 30 minutes, daily; discontinued September 30 1981; Vancouver service did not return until the Mount Baker International in 1995, which began May 26 1995)", "https://www.greatamericanstations.com/stations/bellingham-wa-bel/ (first Bellingham connection to the Great Northern in 1890; the Bellingham Bay and British Columbia Railroad was one of three railroads in the locale around 1891; GN built a brick depot following a track relocation in 1902, which burned a little over 20 years later and was replaced by a single-storey brick structure by local architect F. Stanley Piper; GN used the station until 1969 when Burlington Northern took over; Amtrak used it until the Pacific International was discontinued in 1981; the current Fairhaven Station was renovated from a vintage warehouse in 1995 and serves Amtrak Cascades)"], "geometry_sources": ["Real-traced from OpenStreetMap (Overpass), shortest rail path Seattle King Street Station - Everett - Mount Vernon - Bellingham (Fairhaven) - Blaine - White Rock - New Westminster - Vancouver Pacific Central, routed leg by leg through all eight stations with yard/siding/spur track penalised. NO bridged gaps: the whole route matched to connected active main-line track, and every one of the eight stations sits within 0.5 km of the traced line.", "Traced length 254.01 km / 157.83 mi before simplification, against a documented 154 miles for the GN International and 156 miles for Amtrak's Pacific International -- a close match, the small excess being terminal approach trackage at each end.", "Simplified with Douglas-Peucker at a 20 m tolerance (station points forced as keeps), which is visually indistinguishable at map zooms and keeps the file in line with other layers here.", "The Fairhaven-Blaine feature is the same traced line cut at the Bellingham and Blaine station marks. NOTE this is the MODERN alignment, which is not exactly the 1890 one -- GN relocated the track through Bellingham in 1902, and the F&S also built Fairhaven-Sedro and the Sedro-Cokedale coal branch, neither of which is drawn here."], "not_yet_sourced": "Frequencies are estimates except where noted. The two speed figures taken from sources are averages including stops, not track speed. Also unsourced: the exact 1890 Fairhaven-Blaine alignment as distinct from today's post-1902 track; the Bellingham Bay and British Columbia Railroad (1883/1884, Whatcom to Sumas, connecting with the Canadian Pacific in 1891) which is NOT drawn here at all and would be Bellingham's other pioneer road; the Bellingham Bay and Eastern to Lake Whatcom; the Seattle-Everett-Bellingham interurban; when Mount Vernon and Everett stops were first served; and the exact present-day Cascades frequency, which has changed repeatedly (service to Vancouver was suspended and later restored in the 2020s).", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Fairhaven and Southern Railroad", "line": "Fairhaven and Southern: Fairhaven (Bellingham) \u2013 Blaine", "id": "fairhaven-southern-fairhaven-blaine", "color": "#9E4A2F", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 30, "speed_source": "estimated for an 1890 local road; the one documented F&S timing is 50 minutes Fairhaven-Sedro, a segment not drawn here; unsourced for this segment", "segments": [{"start": 1890, "end": 1891, "headway_min": 720, "note": "Nelson Bennett's Fairhaven and Southern, incorporated November 27 1888, began running from Fairhaven on February 1 1890 and pushed north to the Canadian border at Blaine between August 16 and October 25 1890. The Great Northern had already acquired the franchise in June 1890. For a few months this was a local road that reached the border but had no connection south -- that came with the golden spike at Fairhaven on February 14 1891. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairhaven_and_Southern_Railroad", "https://www.historylink.org/File/8688", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_(GN_train", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacific_International", "https://www.greatamericanstations.com/stations/bellingham-wa-bel/"]}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-122.510282, 48.720985], [-122.506318, 48.722821], [-122.505777, 48.723997], [-122.506008, 48.727087], [-122.505512, 48.72827], [-122.489505, 48.742204], [-122.486822, 48.74591], [-122.482969, 48.748863], [-122.482603, 48.749892], [-122.482855, 48.750892], [-122.484056, 48.752009], [-122.492749, 48.757362], [-122.513823, 48.763545], [-122.518686, 48.765323], [-122.522274, 48.766223], [-122.526199, 48.767817], [-122.527904, 48.768998], [-122.534238, 48.775899], [-122.53836, 48.777384], [-122.54131, 48.779622], [-122.545708, 48.780535], [-122.549758, 48.784038], [-122.56057, 48.788865], [-122.562686, 48.790453], [-122.563556, 48.791995], [-122.566341, 48.801776], [-122.56609, 48.803665], [-122.56294, 48.811698], [-122.563205, 48.813661], [-122.577963, 48.838745], [-122.580098, 48.840745], [-122.589485, 48.846769], [-122.590501, 48.848192], [-122.589601, 48.879402], [-122.589851, 48.881455], [-122.591191, 48.883752], [-122.594126, 48.886155], [-122.74003, 48.983122], [-122.742396, 48.984366], [-122.746634, 48.985452], [-122.750477, 48.987123], [-122.752188, 48.989392], [-122.752521, 48.994133]]}}, {"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Great Northern Railway", "line": "Great Northern: Seattle \u2013 Vancouver BC", "id": "gn-seattle-vancouver", "co